# THE RULE THIS FILE FOLLOWS
#
# Every path in this repo falls into exactly one of three categories. Deciding
# which, before adding a service, is the point — the entries below are just the
# consequence.
#
#   1. COMMITTED CONFIG — non-secret settings that make the stack portable.
#      A .env holding ports, hostnames and image tags belongs in git: clone the
#      repo on another machine and it stands up the same way. Say so explicitly
#      in a comment when it is not obvious, because "a committed .env" looks
#      like a mistake to anyone reading quickly.
#
#   2. MACHINE-LOCAL STATE — real but not portable. Container data directories,
#      per-machine registrations, build artifacts. Ignored, and never a thing
#      another clone needs.
#
#   3. SECRETS — never committed, on any branch, ever. Unseal keys, service
#      account credentials, private keys, tokens. If one lands in history,
#      rotating the secret is the fix; deleting the file is not.
#
# Add each new entry under the heading it belongs to, with a reason if the
# reason is not obvious from the path.

# --- 2. Machine-local state -------------------------------------------------

# Python tooling
.venv/
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
*.swp

# --- 3. Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------

# Belt and braces: catch common secret-shaped names anywhere in the tree, so a
# new service directory is protected before its specific rule is written.
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*_rsa
*.tfvars
